    <string name="vm_parameters_desc">Here are some tweaks on SWAP aggressiveness, memory waterline</string>
    <string name="extra_free_kbytes">extra_free_kbytes(KB)</string>
    <string name="watermark_scale_factor">watermark_scale_factor</string>
    <string name="description">Description</string>
    <string name="ok">OK</string>
    <string name="swappiness_description">Higher value will be more aggressive use of SWAP, this setting has a hard to perceive impact on smoothness, adjust as appropriate~</string>
    <string name="extra_free_kbytes_description">This value specifies how much physical memory the Linux kernel will reserve. When the memory is insufficient, it will immediately begin to compress the memory to SWAP/ZRAM, and the device will obviously become slower before the available memory rises to a reasonable range.

Simply put the extra_free_kbytes setting more Larger, the more aggressive the memory recovery (swap or kill process), but it is not recommended to set too large, so as not to waste physical memory and increase the burden on the disk/processor!</string>
    <string name="watermark_scale_factor_description">The value range is 0~1000, where 1000 means 10%, which means that the difference between the three values of the memory water level line low/min/high (physical RAM capacity) is 10%.

For example (my phone):
pageSize=4KB
managed=1951743(pages)
min_free_kbytes=10900(KB)
watermark_scale_factor=500

The min value is
10900(KB)/4 (PageSize)=2725(pages)
low value is approximately
1951743*5%+2725≈100312(pages)
high value is approximately
1951743*10%+2725≈197899(pages)
Note: the final low, high The value may be too large due to extra_free_kbytes.

In this example,
min=2725≈10MB
low=100312≈392MB
high=197899≈773MB

means that the memory will start to be recycled if the memory is lower than 392M RAM, until the available RAM is higher than 773MB~</string>
